      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Enzyme replacement therapy (ERT) is a newly approved disease-modifying treatment for hypophosphatasia (HPP), a rare metabolic bone disorder. With an orphan drug and ultra-rare disease, sharing information about responders and non-responders is particularly important, as any one centre's familiarity with its use will be limited. Nearly all published data in infants and very young children with life-threatening HPP are from three small clinical trials that have reported generally positive outcomes. We describe in detail a patient with perinatal HPP for whom treatment with ERT was not successful. Lessons learned from this case can inform clinical decision-making and provide topics for the research agenda. We also discuss practical and ethical challenges related to treatment of an ultra-rare disease with an expensive new medication in a publicly funded healthcare system."},
